From 86d629ec172370c57a95a29fa65208b2bdc5c981 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakshman Turlapati <lakshmantvnm@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 15:03:53 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] fix(22-05): drive extension remove with real input and
 settled activation

Raises the extension smoke's assertion boundary from kEnabled to kComplete by
resolving the two defects that blocked the options/remove stretch. They were
resolved differently, and the difference is deliberate.

Remove click -- genuinely fixed. chrome.management.uninstall() with
showConfirmDialog requires user activation (management_api.cc:743), and for a
chrome://extensions caller extension() is null so show_confirm_dialog is forced
on unconditionally. ExecuteJavaScript() conveys no activation, so the injected
click was untrusted, the uninstall never started, and Service.deleteItem()'s
bare .catch() swallowed the rejection. The click is now a real mouse event sent
through RenderWidgetHost::ForwardMouseEvent(), the same function the macOS input
stack calls, so Blink derives activation from real input instead of being told to
assume it. There is no production ExecuteJavaScriptWithUserGesture() to use, and
the ForTests spelling is rejected by the verify script's own no-bypass guard.

The user-consent gate is untouched and still exercised: the native confirmation
dialog is created, shown, and accepted through DialogDelegate::AcceptDialog().
Two paths are made stricter, not looser -- the dialog must now match the widget
name ExtensionUninstallDialog rather than being the first visible browser-owned
dialog, and the resolved click point must hit-test to the remove button before
any mouse event is sent at it.

Bad-message renderer kill -- NOT fixed, only avoided. Disabling an extension
mid-service-worker-start strands the replacement renderer outside ProcessMap
permanently, and the browser later kills it for "Tried to manipulate an event
listener for an unauthorized extension ID." This is an upstream Chromium race a
developer can reach by toggling an extension off just after a reload, not a
harness pacing artifact, and it is recorded as a product finding. The smoke now
waits for activation to settle before disabling, which is the right precondition
for a lifecycle driver independently of the bug, but closes nothing upstream.

That wait took two attempts. Requiring only RenderProcessHost::IsReady() left the
bad message in 10 of 12 runs and failed 8, because process readiness says nothing
about whether the service worker inside it has started -- which is exactly what
the disable was interrupting. Additionally requiring a started worker via
ProcessManager::GetServiceWorkersForExtension() left it in 0 of 12 and failed 1,
that one an unrelated BUS_ADRALN environment flake.

Named failure reasons are retained rather than collapsed into timeouts: three
bugs in this plan hid behind swallowed errors and surfaced only as silent
bounded-wait expiries.

Verified: verify-phase-22-extension-smoke.sh --focused, exit 0, static no-bypass
guards passed, 26 bootstrap unit + 2 developer-private caller + 4 controller
browser tests, zero retry/skip/failure, .ninja_log and .ninja_deps unchanged.
